// Copyright (C) Shielded Technologies
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
//
// The Backgammon table: two seats, one flat pot, Yacht's dust dice -- the ledger-9 build, whose
// rolls come from the blind VRF (backgammon-dice.compact) instead of a committed seed.
//
// One deployment per game, like the Yacht table (table.compact), and advertised the same way --
// by a second instance of the unchanged Lobby. The winner takes the pot less a 1% rake. No
// doubling cube and no gammons: a win is a win.
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. WHAT THE CHAIN ENFORCES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// ENFORCED, per checker (backgammon-board.compact): there is a checker of yours on the point it
// leaves; a checker on the bar enters before anything else moves; the landing point does not
// hold two or more of the opponent's; a single opponent checker there is hit to the bar;
// bearing off only once all fifteen are home; a die larger than the point bears off only from
// the highest occupied point; the dice used are the dice rolled (doubles: four of one value).
//
// NOT ENFORCED: that a ply plays AS MANY dice as it can, and that when only one of two can be
// played it is the larger. Deciding either needs a search over every order the dice could be
// played in, which is too large for a circuit. The client offers only full legal plies and the
// chain-only verifier reports any ply that broke the rule. `applyPlyStrict` enforces the cheap
// half -- a die left unplayed must be unplayable on the final board -- which closes "just pass".
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. THE TURN IS TWO TRANSACTIONS
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//     filling  two or more have joined    ->  start(now, S, dleq)         (seats them, throws the opening)
//     stage 0  the operator owes a roll   ->  resolveRoll(now, S, dleq)   (the only mid-game time stamp)
//     stage 1  the player owes a move     ->  move(ply, nextQuery)
//
// There is no "ask" step as in Yacht, because the queries are PIPELINED: `move` at ply t posts
// the mover's blinded query for its next roll, at ply t+2, and `join` posts each seat's query
// for ply 1 (only the seat that does not start uses it). So when the operator owes a roll, the
// query it answers is already on chain. See backgammon-dice.compact.
//
// The dice of ply t >= 1 are PRIVATE until the move at t lands: only the mover can unblind the
// operator's answer. The opponent sees the roll with the ply that plays it. The opening throw
// (ply 0) is public from the moment the operator makes it.
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. TIME
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Declared-and-sandwiched, as table.compact section 5: `join` and `resolveRoll` declare `now`,
// pinned within a slack of block time. Everything else compares block time against the stored
// `deadline` with a kernel predicate:
//
//     eliminate (timeout)    stage 1, blockTimeGt(deadline)                  -> opponent wins
//     move                   refused once blockTimeGte(deadline + moveTimeoutSecs)
//     abortTable (operator)  stage 0, blockTimeGt(deadline + tableTimeoutSecs) -> both refunded
//     abortTable (filling)   a table timeout after the latest join            -> slots refunded
//     settle waiver          blockTimeGt(deadline + tableTimeoutSecs)         -> pays, unverified
//
// `move` declares no time, so a very late move could otherwise land just before the operator's
// abort clock ran out and leave it no time to roll. Refusing a move once a whole extra move
// timeout has passed guarantees the operator at least `tableTimeoutSecs - moveTimeoutSecs`, and
// the constructor requires that to exceed the timeout floor. A player that late has been
// eliminable for a full move timeout already.
//
// `abortTable` on the operator's stall requires stage 0 -- the roll is the OPERATOR'S -- so a
// player losing a game cannot stall into a refund: its own silence is answered by `eliminate`.
//
// THE MOVE CLOCK IS STAMPED A SLACK AHEAD, so it is a guaranteed minimum. The operator's roll
// declares `now`, which the chain accepts up to `rollSlackSecs()` behind real block time -- so a
// deadline of `now + moveTimeoutSecs` would let a hostile operator start the player's clock in
// the past and shave up to that slack off it (security-review C1, the Yacht table's reason for a
// 240 s floor on every timeout). Stamping `now + rollSlackSecs() + moveTimeoutSecs` instead means
// the player has AT LEAST `moveTimeoutSecs` from the block its dice landed in, whatever the
// operator declared; an honest operator's declared time trails by a few blocks, so in practice
// it is a little more. That is what lets the move clock be three minutes rather than four.
//
// The roll's slack is 60 s, not the 120 s `join` keeps: only the operator declares it, from its
// own tip read to inclusion, with no wallet prompt in between (the reason for join's 120 s).
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. THE RANDOMNESS RESIDUAL, STATED
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The operator cannot choose or change a roll: its VRF key is sealed before either player
// exists, every answer carries a DLEQ the chain checks against it, and every input is forced.
// It never sees a ply's roll before the mover plays it. It can stall, and a stall ends in a full
// refund with no rake.
//
// If the operator colludes with one player anyway, the pair still cannot see the honest
// player's roll before it is played (it needs that player's secret), nor the colluder's own next
// roll before the honest move in between (that roll hashes the secret the honest move reveals).
// What remains is the opening: the operator knows it as it throws it, and the second joiner of
// such a pair can grind its key at join to pick it. The board is deliberately NOT in any roll:
// it would let a player choose among legal moves to steer the next one.
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 6. JOINS DO NOT CONFLICT
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A proof is bound to every ledger value its transaction READ, and the node refuses it if any of
// them moved (per read, not per write -- docs/order-independent-join.md). So a join that read a
// shared seat counter could never land in the same block as another join. Here a join claims one
// of `slotCount()` pre-inserted SLOTS, at random (the client picks a free one), and reads nothing
// but `phase`, constants and its own slot: two joins to different slots touch nothing in common
// and both land. Two to the same slot both read it, so exactly one lands and the other is refused
// before inclusion, its stake untouched -- the read is the guard, and it is mandatory: without it
// both would land and one player's identity would silently overwrite the other's.
//
// Nothing shared is counted while the table fills -- no seat counter, no pot, no set of keys --
// because counting is a read-modify-write. The operator's `start` reads every slot, seats the
// two EARLIEST joiners (by declared join time, slot breaking ties) as seats 0 and 1, moves their
// join entropies and ply-1 queries into the seats, refunds anyone else who joined in the same
// moment, sets the pot, and throws the opening -- one transaction, the one the operator sent
// anyway.
//
// Consequences, stated: the same secret may sit in two slots (seated twice, it plays itself for
// its own stake -- the old one-seat-per-key set was the growing structure that made concurrent
// joins run out of gas, docs/order-independent-join.md "Probe results"); and a join declares its
// time within `timeSlackSecs()` of block time, so among joiners minutes apart a later one can
// claim to be up to that much earlier. Only a third joiner, before the start, can lose a seat to
// that -- and it is refunded in full.
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. k, AND THE DEPLOY
// -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Seven exported circuits. `move` carries the VRF reveal (a hash-to-curve and two scalar
// multiplications) and sits at k=16 like Yacht's `playerMove` on this line; `start` is k=16 too
// (eight slots read, up to six refunds, the opening's hash-to-curve and DLEQ) and runs once a
// game. `resolveRoll` is only the DLEQ against a query already on chain and sits at k=12, like
// Yacht's `resolveRoll` on this line -- the lower edge of the node's proof-size admission floor
// (table.compact section 3), which k=12 has always cleared. The rest are 13 and 14. Measured
// 2026-09-29 with cli/tools/circuit-k.mjs.

pragma language_version >= 0.26;

import CompactStandardLibrary;

include "dice-core";

include "policy-core";

include "vrf-core";

include "backgammon-dice";

include "backgammon-board";

// ===========================================================================================
// Constants
// ===========================================================================================

/** Slack on a declared `now`, in seconds. Identical to the Yacht table's (security-review C1). */
pure circuit timeSlackSecs(): Uint<64> {
  return 120;
}

/** The table timeout must strictly exceed `timeSlackSecs() * timeoutSlackFactor()` = 240 s. */
pure circuit timeoutSlackFactor(): Uint<64> {
  return 2;
}

/**
 * Slack on the operator's declared `now` in `resolveRoll`. See section 3: the move deadline is
 * stamped this far ahead, so it can never be shaved.
 */
pure circuit rollSlackSecs(): Uint<64> {
  return 60;
}

/** The shortest move clock a table may seal: time to prove, approve and land a move. */
pure circuit minMoveSecs(): Uint<64> {
  return 120;
}

/** Smallest stake: the 1% rake is never zero. */
pure circuit minTier(): Uint<64> {
  return 100;
}

/** Largest stake, 10^15, so `tier * 2` stays far inside `Uint<64>`. */
pure circuit maxTier(): Uint<64> {
  return 1000000000000000;
}

/**
 * Seat slots a join may claim (section 6). More than the two seats so that two players joining
 * at once collide only one time in eight; the start seats two and refunds the rest. Must match
 * the `Vector<8, SlotInfo>` of `allSlots`.
 */
export pure circuit slotCount(): Uint<8> {
  return 8;
}

/** "Nobody": the value of `winner` until someone has won. */
export pure circuit noWinner(): Uint<8> {
  return 2;
}

/** The operator owes the roll. */
export pure circuit stageRoll(): Uint<8> {
  return 0;
}

/** The player to move owes its move. */
export pure circuit stageMove(): Uint<8> {
  return 1;
}

export enum BgPhase {
  filling,
  playing,
  decided,
  settled,
  aborted,
}

/** Both sides, each from its own point of view (see backgammon-board.compact). */
export struct Board {
  s0: Vector<26, Uint<8>>,
  s1: Vector<26, Uint<8>>,
}

/** Written once per seat, at `join`. Pre-inserted as zeros so every lookup is total. */
export struct SeatIdentity {
  addr: UserAddress,
  keyCommit: Bytes<32>,
}

// ===========================================================================================
// Ledger
// ===========================================================================================

/** Unique per table: the domain separator on every roll and entropy hash. */
export sealed ledger tableId: Bytes<32>;

/** Stake per seat. The pot holds exactly `tier * seatCount`. */
export sealed ledger tier: Uint<64>;

export sealed ledger rakeAddress: UserAddress;

/** `x*G` for the table's VRF key `x`, sealed before any player existed. */
export sealed ledger vrfPublicKey: JubjubPoint;

/** How long the player to move has, from the roll, before it can be eliminated. */
export sealed ledger moveTimeoutSecs: Uint<64>;

/** The fill clock, and the operator's grace past a deadline. See section 3. */
export sealed ledger tableTimeoutSecs: Uint<64>;

/** `inviteCommitment(code)` on a private table; zeros on a public one. */
export sealed ledger inviteHash: Bytes<32>;

export ledger phase: BgPhase;

/** 0 while filling (nothing is counted then -- section 6), 2 from the start. */
export ledger seatCount: Uint<8>;

/** The seat whose ply it is. Set by the opening roll; meaningless before. */
export ledger toMove: Uint<8>;

/** `stageRoll()` or `stageMove()`. */
export ledger stage: Uint<8>;

/** Plies played so far. 0 until the opening ply has been played. */
export ledger turn: Uint<16>;

/**
 * The dice of the last ply PLAYED -- written by its move, which is where they become public --
 * or, from the opening throw until the opening ply, the opening pair. Zeros before the throw.
 */
export ledger dice: BgDice;

export ledger board: Board;

/** The last ply played, verbatim, so a client and the verifier can read what happened. */
export ledger lastPly: Ply;

/** See section 3. */
export ledger deadline: Uint<64>;

export ledger pot: Uint<64>;

/** The winning seat once decided; `noWinner()` before. */
export ledger winner: Uint<8>;

/** The VRF key once `settle` showed it opens `vrfPublicKey`; 0 on an unverified settle. */
export ledger revealedSecret: Field;

/** How the table ended, as one hash. Zeros until `settle` or `abortTable`. */
export ledger finalDigest: Bytes<32>;

export ledger seatIdentity: Map<Uint<8>, SeatIdentity>;

/** Each seat's join entropy, which the opening throw hashes. */
export ledger joinEntropy: Map<Uint<8>, Bytes<32>>;

/** A seat's blinded VRF query for its roll at ply `turn`. Checked only when it is used. */
export struct BgQuery {
  turn: Uint<16>,
  blinded: JubjubPoint,
}

/** Each seat's query for its next roll: ply 1 from `join`, then ply t+2 from its move at t. */
export ledger query: Map<Uint<8>, BgQuery>;

/** The operator's answer `S = x*B` to the query of the player to move. See backgammon-dice. */
export ledger rollAnswer: JubjubPoint;

/** The roll secret the last move revealed; it goes into the next roll. Zeros before ply 0. */
export ledger lastSecret: Bytes<32>;

/** The filling table's slots (section 6): who joined each, its e(1), and its declared time. */
/**
 * One slot of a filling table (section 6): who joined it, the join entropy it revealed (which the
 * opening hashes), its blinded query for ply 1, and when it declared it joined.
 */
export struct BgSlot {
  addr: UserAddress,
  keyCommit: Bytes<32>,
  entropy: Bytes<32>,
  query: JubjubPoint,
  joinedAt: Uint<64>,
}

/** The filling table's slots. ONE map of records, not four maps: see the constructor. */
export ledger joinSlots: Map<Uint<8>, BgSlot>;

/** Sink for the transaction padding. See table.compact section 3. */
export ledger padStore: Bytes<4096>;

pure circuit paddingConstant(): Bytes<4096> {
  return pad(4096, "dust-dice:bg:v1:pad");
}

// ===========================================================================================
// Witnesses -- a subset of the Yacht table's (src/table-witnesses.ts serves both)
// ===========================================================================================

/** The seated player's secret `sk`. `join` commits to it; `move` proves it. */
witness playerEntropySecret(): Bytes<32>;

/** A private table's invite code. Zeros otherwise. */
witness inviteCode(): Bytes<32>;

/**
 * The unblinded VRF output of the mover's roll, `Gamma = rho^-1 * S = x*P`. Checked by
 * `S == rho*Gamma` with `rho` derived in the circuit. See backgammon-dice.compact.
 */
witness vrfGamma(): JubjubPoint;

// ===========================================================================================
// Helpers
// ===========================================================================================

/** The closing certificate. See table.compact's `FinalEvent`; this one also binds the board. */
export struct BgFinalEvent {
  domain: Bytes<32>,
  tableId: Bytes<32>,
  ending: Uint<8>,
  winner: Uint<8>,
  turn: Uint<16>,
  paid: Uint<64>,
  rake: Uint<64>,
  verifiable: Boolean,
  board: Board,
}

pure circuit finalDigestOf(
               id: Bytes<32>,
               ending: Uint<8>,
               win: Uint<8>,
               plies: Uint<16>,
               paid: Uint<64>,
               rake: Uint<64>,
               verifiable: Boolean,
               b: Board,
               ): Bytes<32> {
  return persistentHash<BgFinalEvent>(
           BgFinalEvent { domain: pad(32, "dust-dice:bg:v1:final"),
                          tableId: id,
                          ending: ending,
                          winner: win,
                          turn: plies,
                          paid: paid,
                          rake: rake,
                          verifiable: verifiable,
                          board: b, }
           );
}

/** Rake split: `q * 100 + r == pot`, `r < 100`. Returns `[winnerPayout, rake]`. */
pure circuit bgSplitPot(total: Uint<64>, q: Uint<64>, r: Uint<64>): Vector<2, Uint<64>> {
  assert((q * 100 + r == total) && (r < 100),
         "rake split must satisfy q*100 + r == pot with r < 100"
         );
  return [(total - q) as Uint<64>, q];
}

/** Must be the last thing every exported circuit does. See table.compact's `padTransaction`. */
circuit padTransaction(): [] {
  kernel.checkpoint();
  padStore = paddingConstant();
}

/**
 * BALLAST: make `resolveRoll` heavy enough that ledger 9 classes it wholly FALLIBLE.
 *
 * Load-bearing, as table.compact's `resolveBallast` is for Yacht's resolve. The one-transaction
 * ply merges the player's `move` (heavy, wholly fallible) with the operator's `resolveRoll` for the
 * next ply, and ledger 9 refuses a merged transaction in which a call with a GUARANTEED
 * transcript follows one with a FALLIBLE one (`CausalityConstraintViolation`, bugs-found #35).
 * Left alone, the roll -- a DLEQ proven off chain and a handful of cells -- is light enough to be
 * classed guaranteed. The weight is reads of slot 0's join time, which nothing writes once the
 * table has started (only joins and leaves, while filling), so it binds to nothing a ply races.
 * cli/src/bg/ply.ts checks the classification on every roll it builds.
 */
circuit resolveBallast(): [] {
  for (const _i of 0..10) {
    assert(joinSlots.member(0), "resolveRoll: slot 0 is always present");
  }
}

/** Pin a declared (already disclosed) `now` to block time. See table.compact's `pinTime`. */
circuit pinTime(now: Uint<64>): [] {
  assert(blockTimeGte(now), "declared time is ahead of block time");
  assert(blockTimeLt((now + timeSlackSecs()) as Uint<64>),
         "declared time is further behind block time than the allowed slack"
         );
}

/** `pinTime` with the operator's tighter roll slack. */
circuit pinRollTime(now: Uint<64>): [] {
  assert(blockTimeGte(now), "declared time is ahead of block time");
  assert(blockTimeLt((now + rollSlackSecs()) as Uint<64>),
         "declared time is further behind block time than the roll's slack"
         );
}

/** Every stake received is still in the pot: nothing leaves before the end. */
circuit assertCustody(): [] {
  assert(pot == (tier * (seatCount as Uint<64>)) as Uint<64>,
         "custody invariant broken: pot != tier * seatCount"
         );
}

/** Prove knowledge of seat `s`'s secret. */
circuit proveSeat(s: Uint<8>): Bytes<32> {
  const sk = playerEntropySecret();
  assert(disclose(entropyKeyCommitment(tableId, sk) == seatIdentity.lookup(s).keyCommit),
         "wrong entropy secret for this seat"
         );
  return sk;
}

/** One slot, as the start and the filling abort read it. */
export struct SlotInfo {
  slot: Uint<8>,
  occupied: Boolean,
  joinedAt: Uint<64>,
  addr: UserAddress,
}

/** A free slot: the zero address marks it free (a join refuses a zero payout address). */
pure circuit emptySlot(): BgSlot {
  return BgSlot { addr: default<UserAddress>,
                  keyCommit: default<Bytes<32>>,
                  entropy: default<Bytes<32>>,
                  query: default<JubjubPoint>,
                  joinedAt: 0, };
}

/** A slot's holder, as a seat identity. */
circuit slotSeat(i: Uint<8>): SeatIdentity {
  const sl = joinSlots.lookup(i);
  return SeatIdentity { addr: sl.addr, keyCommit: sl.keyCommit };
}

circuit slotInfo(i: Uint<8>): SlotInfo {
  const id = joinSlots.lookup(i);
  return SlotInfo { slot: i,
                    occupied: id.addr != default<UserAddress>,
                    joinedAt: id.joinedAt,
                    addr: id.addr, };
}

/** Every slot. Read only by the start and the filling abort -- never by a join. */
circuit allSlots(): Vector<8, SlotInfo> {
  return [slotInfo(0), slotInfo(1), slotInfo(2), slotInfo(3),
          slotInfo(4), slotInfo(5), slotInfo(6), slotInfo(7)];
}

/** The two earliest joiners, how many joined, and when the last did. */
export struct Pick2 {
  count: Uint<8>,
  a: Uint<8>,
  aKey: Uint<64>,
  b: Uint<8>,
  bKey: Uint<64>,
  latest: Uint<64>,
}

pure circuit noPick2(): Pick2 {
  return Pick2 { count: 0, a: 0, aKey: 18446744073709551615, b: 0, bKey: 18446744073709551615,
                 latest: 0 };
}

/** Earlier declared join first; the slot index breaks a tie, so the order is total. */
pure circuit pickTwo(acc: Pick2, s: SlotInfo): Pick2 {
  const key = (s.joinedAt * 16 + s.slot) as Uint<64>;
  const beforeA = key < acc.aKey;
  const beforeB = key < acc.bKey;
  return !s.occupied
           ? acc
           : Pick2 { count: (acc.count + 1) as Uint<8>,
                     a: beforeA ? s.slot : acc.a,
                     aKey: beforeA ? key : acc.aKey,
                     b: beforeA ? acc.a : (beforeB ? s.slot : acc.b),
                     bKey: beforeA ? acc.aKey : (beforeB ? key : acc.bKey),
                     latest: s.joinedAt > acc.latest ? s.joinedAt : acc.latest, };
}

/** Refund every occupied slot, except the two seated ones when `keep`. */
circuit refundSlots(slots: Vector<8, SlotInfo>, keepA: Uint<8>, keepB: Uint<8>, keep: Boolean): [] {
  for (const s of slots) {
    if (s.occupied && !(keep && (s.slot == keepA || s.slot == keepB))) {
      sendUnshielded(nativeToken(), tier as Uint<128>, right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(s.addr));
    }
  }
}

// ===========================================================================================
// Constructor
// ===========================================================================================

constructor(id: Bytes<32>,
            stake: Uint<64>,
            rakeTo: UserAddress,
            vrfPk: JubjubPoint,
            moveTimeout: Uint<64>,
            tableTimeout: Uint<64>,
            invite: Bytes<32>,
            ) {
  assert(stake >= minTier(), "tier must be at least 100 so the 1% rake is non-zero");
  assert(stake <= maxTier(), "tier is above the maximum a table may stake");
  const floor = (timeSlackSecs() * timeoutSlackFactor()) as Uint<64>;
  assert(moveTimeout >= minMoveSecs(), "move timeout must be at least two minutes");
  assert(tableTimeout > floor, "table timeout must exceed the declared-time floor");
  // Section 3: after the latest move `move` accepts, the operator still has
  // `tableTimeout - moveTimeout` to roll before it can be aborted; that must beat the floor too.
  assert(tableTimeout > (moveTimeout + floor) as Uint<64>,
         "table timeout must exceed the move timeout by the declared-time floor"
         );

  tableId = disclose(id);
  tier = disclose(stake);
  rakeAddress = disclose(rakeTo);
  vrfPublicKey = disclose(vrfPk);
  moveTimeoutSecs = disclose(moveTimeout);
  tableTimeoutSecs = disclose(tableTimeout);
  inviteHash = disclose(invite);

  phase = BgPhase.filling;
  seatCount = 0;
  toMove = 0;
  stage = stageRoll();
  turn = 0;
  dice = BgDice { a: 0, b: 0 };
  board = Board { s0: bgInitialSide(), s1: bgInitialSide() };
  lastPly = default<Ply>;
  // Stays 0 until the start: a filling table's clock is its latest join (see `abortTable`).
  deadline = 0;
  pot = 0;
  winner = noWinner();
  revealedSecret = 0 as Field;
  finalDigest = default<Bytes<32>>;
  rollAnswer = default<JubjubPoint>;
  lastSecret = default<Bytes<32>>;

  // Both slots pre-inserted, so every lookup at a fixed index is total (table.compact section 7).
  for (const s of 0..2) {
    seatIdentity.insert(
      s as Uint<8>,
      SeatIdentity { addr: default<UserAddress>, keyCommit: default<Bytes<32>> }
      );
    joinEntropy.insert(s as Uint<8>, default<Bytes<32>>);
    query.insert(s as Uint<8>, BgQuery { turn: 0, blinded: default<JubjubPoint> });
  }
  // Every slot pre-inserted: a join then overwrites one cell of a fixed size, so its cost does
  // not depend on how many others joined first (the gas trap of docs/order-independent-join.md).
  //
  // ONE MAP OF RECORDS, NOT FOUR MAPS. On this ledger a slot also carries its ply-1 query, and
  // with identity, entropy, query and join time in four maps of eight the deploy was refused
  // ("Transaction would exhaust the block limits", devnet 2026-09-29: 32,345 bytes written
  // against the Yacht table's 28,920, which lands). One record per slot is 25,569 -- each map
  // entry carries its own overhead -- and the start and the filling abort read eight cells
  // instead of sixteen.
  for (const s of 0..8) {
    joinSlots.insert(s as Uint<8>, emptySlot());
  }
}

// ===========================================================================================
// join
// ===========================================================================================

/**
 * Claim `slot`, stake `tier`, register the seat key, reveal its join entropy (which the opening
 * hashes) and post its blinded query for a roll at ply 1 (only the seat that does not start
 * uses it).
 *
 * Reads `phase`, constants and this slot, and nothing else (section 6), so joins to different
 * slots land together. The game starts with the operator's opening roll, not with a join: a join
 * cannot know it is the second without reading what the other joins wrote.
 */
export circuit join(slot: Uint<8>,
                    payoutTo: UserAddress,
                    now: Uint<64>,
                    firstQuery: JubjubPoint,
                    ): Uint<8> {
  assert(phase == BgPhase.filling, "join: table is not filling");

  const s = disclose(slot);
  const t = disclose(now);
  const who = disclose(payoutTo);
  assert(s < slotCount(), "join: no such seat");
  pinTime(t);
  assert(who != default<UserAddress>, "join: payout address must not be the zero address");
  // THE GUARD: two joins to one slot both read it, so exactly one lands.
  assert(joinSlots.lookup(s).addr == default<UserAddress>,
         "join: that seat was taken a moment ago -- pick another"
         );

  const sk = playerEntropySecret();
  const commit = disclose(entropyKeyCommitment(tableId, sk));

  const code = inviteCode();
  assert(disclose(inviteHash == default<Bytes<32>> || inviteCommitment(code) == inviteHash),
         "join: this table is private -- the invite code does not match"
         );

  joinSlots.insert(s, BgSlot { addr: who,
                           keyCommit: commit,
                           entropy: disclose(bgJoinEntropy(sk, tableId)),
                           query: disclose(firstQuery),
                           joinedAt: t, });
  receiveUnshielded(nativeToken(), tier as Uint<128>);

  padTransaction();
  return s;
}

// ===========================================================================================
// start -- the operator seats the players and throws the opening
// ===========================================================================================

/**
 * The START (section 6): seat the two earliest joiners, refund anyone else who joined in the same
 * moment, set the pot, and throw the opening -- one transaction.
 *
 * The opening's point is public, `bgOpeningPoint` of the two seated joiners' join entropies, so
 * the dice follow from the operator's `S` here: the seat with the higher die moves first and
 * plays both numbers. The DLEQ against the sealed `vrfPublicKey` is the authority, as at every
 * roll.
 *
 * A circuit of its own, not a branch of `resolveRoll`: reading eight slots and refunding up to six
 * of them put `resolveRoll` at k=16 (measured 2026-09-29), and every ply's roll would have paid
 * for a start that happens once. Here it is paid once.
 */
export circuit start(now: Uint<64>,
                     response: JubjubPoint,
                     dleqA1: JubjubPoint,
                     dleqA2: JubjubPoint,
                     dleqZ: Field,
                     ): BgDice {
  assert(phase == BgPhase.filling, "start: table is not filling");

  const t = disclose(now);
  pinRollTime(t);

  const slots = allSlots();
  const p = fold(pickTwo, noPick2(), slots);
  assert(p.count >= 2, "start: fewer than two players have joined");
  const sa = joinSlots.lookup(p.a);
  const sb = joinSlots.lookup(p.b);
  const ea = sa.entropy;
  const eb = sb.entropy;
  seatIdentity.insert(0, SeatIdentity { addr: sa.addr, keyCommit: sa.keyCommit });
  seatIdentity.insert(1, SeatIdentity { addr: sb.addr, keyCommit: sb.keyCommit });
  joinEntropy.insert(0, ea);
  joinEntropy.insert(1, eb);
  query.insert(0, BgQuery { turn: 1, blinded: sa.query });
  query.insert(1, BgQuery { turn: 1, blinded: sb.query });
  refundSlots(slots, p.a, p.b, true);
  pot = (tier * 2) as Uint<64>;
  seatCount = 2;
  phase = BgPhase.playing;

  const s = disclose(response);
  assert(disclose(vrfDleqHolds(vrfPublicKey, bgOpeningPoint(tableId, ea, eb), s, dleqA1, dleqA2,
                               dleqZ)),
         "start: the answer is not this table's VRF applied to the opening"
         );
  const thrown = bgDice(tableId, s, default<Bytes<32>>, 0, true);
  toMove = thrown.a > thrown.b ? 0 : 1;
  dice = thrown;
  rollAnswer = s;
  stage = stageMove();
  // A slack ahead of the declared time: at least `moveTimeoutSecs` of real time (section 3).
  deadline = (t + rollSlackSecs() + moveTimeoutSecs) as Uint<64>;

  padTransaction();
  return thrown;
}

// ===========================================================================================
// resolveRoll -- the operator
// ===========================================================================================

/**
 * Answer the mover's blinded query for the ply about to be played (ply 1 on; the opening is
 * `start`'s).
 *
 * The DLEQ against the sealed `vrfPublicKey` is the authority -- exactly one `S` has a valid
 * proof for a given point, so the operator can neither choose the answer nor aim it. The point
 * is blind, and the dice appear only in the mover's `move`.
 */
export circuit resolveRoll(now: Uint<64>,
                           response: JubjubPoint,
                           dleqA1: JubjubPoint,
                           dleqA2: JubjubPoint,
                           dleqZ: Field,
                           ): [] {
  assert(phase == BgPhase.playing, "resolveRoll: table is not playing");
  assert(stage == stageRoll(), "resolveRoll: no roll is owed");

  const t = disclose(now);
  pinRollTime(t);

  const q = query.lookup(toMove);
  assert(q.turn == turn, "resolveRoll: the player to move has no query for this ply");
  const s = disclose(response);
  assert(disclose(vrfDleqHolds(vrfPublicKey, q.blinded, s, dleqA1, dleqA2, dleqZ)),
         "resolveRoll: the answer is not this table's VRF applied to the query"
         );

  rollAnswer = s;
  stage = stageMove();
  // A slack ahead of the declared time: at least `moveTimeoutSecs` of real time (section 3).
  deadline = (t + rollSlackSecs() + moveTimeoutSecs) as Uint<64>;

  // Before the padding's checkpoint, as Yacht's. See `resolveBallast`.
  resolveBallast();
  padTransaction();
}

// ===========================================================================================
// move -- the player to move
// ===========================================================================================

/**
 * Reveal the roll and play it: up to four sub-moves, in order, each checked against the board as
 * it stands when that checker moves. `count == 0` is a pass (no legal move -- see section 1).
 *
 * At ply 0 the dice are the opening throw, already public. After it they are the mover's VRF
 * roll, unblinded here -- the only place anyone but the mover can learn them. The move reveals
 * the roll's secret (the next roll hashes it) and posts the mover's query for its roll at ply
 * t+2. Bearing off the fifteenth checker decides the game for the mover.
 */
export circuit move(ply: Vector<4, SubMove>, count: Uint<8>, nextQuery: JubjubPoint): Uint<16> {
  assert(phase == BgPhase.playing, "move: table is not playing");
  assert(stage == stageMove(), "move: the dice have not been rolled");
  assert(blockTimeLt((deadline + moveTimeoutSecs) as Uint<64>),
         "move: too late -- a whole move timeout has passed since the deadline"
         );

  const mover = toMove;
  const sk = proveSeat(mover);
  const n = turn;
  const opening = n == 0;

  // ---- the roll (backgammon-dice.compact) ----------------------------------------------------
  //
  //   1. The query the operator answered is this seat's, for this ply.
  //   2. `P` is rebuilt from this seat's forced secret for this ply -- not a point of its choice.
  //   3. `B == rho*P`, with `rho` derived here, ties the answered query to that `P`.
  //   4. `S == rho*Gamma` unblinds it without an inverse. It pins Gamma up to a small-order
  //      point, which the dice never see: they hash `8*Gamma`.
  const v = bgRollSecret(sk, tableId, n);
  const rho = bgBlindingFor(sk, tableId, n) as JubjubScalar;
  const q = query.lookup(mover);
  assert(opening || q.turn == n, "move: this seat has no query on chain for this ply");
  assert(disclose(opening || ecMul(bgVrfInputPoint(tableId, n, v), rho) == q.blinded),
         "move: the query on chain is not this seat's input point blinded"
         );
  const gamma = vrfGamma();
  assert(disclose(opening || ecMul(gamma, rho) == rollAnswer),
         "move: gamma does not unblind the operator's answer"
         );
  const rolled = bgDice(tableId, gamma, lastSecret, n, false);
  // The one disclosure of the roll: the dice are public from this move on, `gamma` never.
  const d = disclose(opening ? dice : rolled);

  const p = disclose(ply);
  const c = disclose(count);
  const bd = board;
  const first = mover == 0;
  const out = applyPlyStrict(first ? bd.s0 : bd.s1, first ? bd.s1 : bd.s0, d.a, d.b, p, c);

  board = first ? Board { s0: out.mine, s1: out.opp } : Board { s0: out.opp, s1: out.mine };
  lastPly = Ply { moves: p, count: c };
  dice = d;
  lastSecret = disclose(v);
  query.insert(mover, BgQuery { turn: (n + 2) as Uint<16>, blinded: disclose(nextQuery) });
  const next = (n + 1) as Uint<16>;
  turn = next;

  if (out.won) {
    phase = BgPhase.decided;
    winner = mover;
  } else {
    toMove = (1 - mover) as Uint<8>;
    stage = stageRoll();
  }

  padTransaction();
  return next;
}

// ===========================================================================================
// eliminate -- a timeout, a resignation, or leaving a filling table
// ===========================================================================================

/**
 * Three things, by state:
 *
 *   * playing, not voluntary: the seat to move owes its move past the deadline -> the opponent
 *     wins. Permissionless.
 *   * playing, voluntary: the seat resigns (proves its secret) -> the opponent wins.
 *   * filling, voluntary: the only seat leaves and is refunded in full; the table is empty again.
 */
export circuit eliminate(seat: Uint<8>, voluntary: Boolean): Uint<8> {
  const s = disclose(seat);
  const vol = disclose(voluntary);
  const filling = phase == BgPhase.filling;
  const playing = phase == BgPhase.playing;
  // While filling, `seat` names a SLOT (leaving it); in play, seat 0 or 1.
  assert(filling ? s < slotCount() : s < seatCount, "eliminate: no such seat");

  const sk = playerEntropySecret();
  const who = filling ? slotSeat(s < slotCount() ? s : 0)
                      : seatIdentity.lookup(s < 2 ? s : 0);
  assert(!filling || who.addr != default<UserAddress>, "eliminate: that seat is empty");
  assert(disclose(!vol || entropyKeyCommitment(tableId, sk) == who.keyCommit),
         "eliminate: wrong entropy secret for this seat"
         );

  const timedOut = playing && !vol && stage == stageMove() && s == toMove && blockTimeGt(deadline);
  assert((filling && vol) || (playing && vol) || timedOut,
         "eliminate: this seat is not past its deadline, and is not leaving of its own accord"
         );

  if (filling) {
    // Leaving empties the slot for someone else, and reads nothing another join writes.
    sendUnshielded(nativeToken(), tier as Uint<128>, right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(who.addr));
    joinSlots.insert(s, emptySlot());
  } else {
    winner = (1 - s) as Uint<8>;
    phase = BgPhase.decided;
  }

  padTransaction();
  return s;
}

// ===========================================================================================
// settle -- pay the winner
// ===========================================================================================

/**
 * Pay the winner the pot less the 1% rake, and reveal the VRF key.
 *
 * Every roll was already proven as it was made (the DLEQ at each resolve, the unblinding in each
 * move). The key is what lets anyone REPLAY them: with `x` and the roll secrets the moves
 * revealed, each `Gamma = x*P` and so each roll can be recomputed from the public log alone.
 *
 * Past `deadline + tableTimeoutSecs` the key is waived: the winner is paid regardless and
 * `revealedSecret` stays 0, the table's "paid but not replayable" marker. A withheld key can
 * therefore delay a payout, never cancel it.
 */
export circuit settle(vrfSecret: Field, q: Uint<64>, r: Uint<64>): Uint<8> {
  assert(phase == BgPhase.decided, "settle: the game is not decided");

  const x = disclose(vrfSecret);
  const opens = ecMulGenerator(x as JubjubScalar) == vrfPublicKey;
  const graceExpired = blockTimeGt((deadline + tableTimeoutSecs) as Uint<64>);
  assert(opens || graceExpired, "settle: the secret does not open the table's VRF key");

  assertCustody();

  const w = winner;
  const split = bgSplitPot(pot, disclose(q), disclose(r));
  sendUnshielded(
    nativeToken(),
    split[0] as Uint<128>,
    right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(seatIdentity.lookup(w).addr)
    );
  sendUnshielded(
    nativeToken(),
    split[1] as Uint<128>,
    right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(rakeAddress)
    );

  pot = 0;
  revealedSecret = opens ? x : 0 as Field;
  finalDigest =
    finalDigestOf(tableId, BgPhase.settled as Uint<8>, w, turn, split[0], split[1], opens, board);
  phase = BgPhase.settled;

  padTransaction();
  return w;
}

// ===========================================================================================
// abortTable -- refund a table that cannot finish
// ===========================================================================================

/**
 * Permissionless. Refunds every stake in full, no rake, in two situations:
 *
 *   * filling with one seat, past the fill clock;
 *   * playing, the OPERATOR owes the roll, and the table grace has passed on top of the
 *     deadline. Requiring stage 0 is load-bearing: see section 3.
 */
export circuit abortTable(): Uint<64> {
  const filling = phase == BgPhase.filling;
  const slots = allSlots();
  const p = fold(pickTwo, noPick2(), slots);
  // Filling: nobody new has joined for a whole table timeout -- a lone player waiting, or a full
  // table the operator never started (section 6).
  const fillingStalled =
          filling && p.count > 0 && blockTimeGt((p.latest + tableTimeoutSecs) as Uint<64>);
  const operatorStalled =
          phase == BgPhase.playing &&
          stage == stageRoll() &&
          blockTimeGt((deadline + tableTimeoutSecs) as Uint<64>);
  assert(fillingStalled || operatorStalled,
         "abortTable: the table is not stalled past its deadline"
         );

  // While filling, nothing counted the stakes (section 6): the slots are the account of them.
  // After the start the seated players are still in their slots, so those are refunded as SEATS
  // and the slots are not touched -- refunding both would pay them twice.
  assertCustody();
  if (filling) {
    refundSlots(slots, 0, 0, false);
  } else {
    sendUnshielded(
      nativeToken(),
      tier as Uint<128>,
      right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(seatIdentity.lookup(0).addr)
      );
    sendUnshielded(
      nativeToken(),
      tier as Uint<128>,
      right<ContractAddress, UserAddress>(seatIdentity.lookup(1).addr)
      );
  }

  const refunded = filling ? (tier * (p.count as Uint<64>)) as Uint<64> : pot;
  pot = 0;
  finalDigest =
    finalDigestOf(tableId, BgPhase.aborted as Uint<8>, noWinner(), turn, 0, 0, false, board);
  phase = BgPhase.aborted;

  padTransaction();
  return refunded;
}

export { UserAddress, ContractAddress };
